  "concise_summary": "PKD synthesizes his 2-3-74 mystical experience through Tillich and Boehme's dialectical theology: God's essence consists of an internal dialectic between the irrational prerational will (darkness/madness) and Logos (reason/sanity), with Logos inevitably victorious in each dialectical sequence. He argues that VALIS depicts this divine drama both cosmically (in world history, including Nixon's fall) and individually (in Horselover Fat's cure), and that his vision revealed a new theological framework: reality as dynamic, process-driven divine self-reconciliation rather than static being.",
  "key_claims": [
    "The dialectic between prerational will and Logos constitutes God's essential nature, not a conflict between God and Satan",
    "Logos always wins in each dialectical sequence because it possesses absolute a priori knowledge while the dark side has only contingent a posteriori knowledge",
    "PKD's 2-74 experience was a meta-abstraction and synthesis in the Hegelian sense: the reconciliation of opposites into a higher consciousness, equivalent to spiritual justification or Christification",
    "The novel VALIS dramatizes this dialectic both macrocosmically (world history, the overthrow of Nixon) and microcosmically (Horselover Fat's madness and cure through Sophia/Logos)",
    "God and world are homologized through this dialectic: the same dynamic process operates in self, world, and God simultaneously, collapsing the inner-outer distinction",
    "Logos as reason is explicitly identified with Hagia Sophia and with Christ, making the vision a theophany of divine rationality as the principle of all change and becoming"
  ],
  "recurring_concepts": [
    "The dialectic (Boehme's internal conflict within God)",
    "Logos (divine reason; Christ; Hagia Sophia; the bright side)",
    "Prerational will (the dark side; madness; creative irrationality)",
    "Meta-abstraction (PKD's term for his synthesis experience; equivalent to Hegelian reconciliation)",
    "VALIS (the novel; the vision; the divine principle of becoming)",
    "Horselover Fat (the protagonist; the microcosm; the locus of the dialectic's victory)",
    "Process theology (the framework uniting Boehme, Hegel, Hartshorne, Whitehead)",
    "Homologization (the collapse of distinctions between self, world, God)"
  ],
  "people_entities": [
    "Philip K. Dick (author; visionary; the consciousness that experienced 2-3-74)",
    "Jakob Boehme (medieval mystic and theologian; precursor to PKD's dialectical vision)",
    "Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel (philosopher; source of synthesis, reconciliation, absolute spirit)",
    "Paul Tillich (Protestant theologian; source of dialectical framework)",
    "Horselover Fat (protagonist of VALIS; PKD's fictional alter ego; bearer of the vision)",
